# Port Deprecated Definitions

When you introduce a **replacement** for an existing export (type, function, component, constant, or module path), treat deprecation and migration as part of the same task—not a follow-up.

## Workflow

1. **Add the new definition** in the canonical module (or move logic there).
2. **Mark the old symbol deprecated** with a JSDoc tag that names the replacement:
   - `/** @deprecated Use {@link NewName}. */` for types and values
   - `/** @deprecated Use NewName */` when a link is unnecessary
3. **Keep legacy symbols as thin aliases** during transition (e.g. `export type Old = New`) so existing imports still compile but IDEs and `tsc` surface warnings.
4. **Port all in-repo callers** to the new name in the same change when the blast radius is reasonable; search the repo before finishing.
5. **Update barrel exports** (`index.ts`, public re-exports) to prefer the new symbol; re-export deprecated names only if external consumers still need them.
6. **Remove deprecated shims** once nothing references them (grep again before deleting).

## Do

- Migrate imports, props, and return types to the new API—not only the definition site.
- Rename related helpers, files, or routes when the product name changed (stay consistent end-to-end).
- Use `@link` in deprecation comments when the replacement lives in the same file or is easy to discover.

## In-repo scope

When the blast radius is in this repository, **port callers and remove the old symbol in the same change**. Do not leave long-lived deprecated aliases that only rename types (`export type Old = New`) once zero in-repo callers remain. Thin aliases are for brief transition mid-PR or documented external packages — not permanent dual homes.

## Avoid

- Leaving most call sites on deprecated names while only adding the new export.
- Introducing a second parallel implementation instead of delegating the old symbol to the new one.
- Deleting the old symbol in the same PR without porting callers (unless the symbol was unused).
- Keeping in-repo-only `@deprecated` shims across PRs when callers could have been ported.

## Examples

```ts
// ✅ GOOD — new canonical type, deprecated alias, callers updated
export type AchievementFeedItemViewModel = { /* ... */ };

/** @deprecated Use {@link AchievementFeedItemViewModel}. */
export type FeedRow = AchievementFeedItemViewModel;
```

```ts
// ❌ BAD — new type added, old name still used everywhere, no @deprecated
export type AchievementFeedItemViewModel = { /* ... */ };
export type FeedRow = { /* duplicate shape */ };
```

```tsx
// ✅ GOOD — component renamed; old name deprecated and delegates
/** @deprecated Use {@link AddToHomeScreenInstallBlock}. */
export function LegacyInstallPrompt(props: Props) {
  return <AddToHomeScreenInstallBlock {...props} />;
}
```

Before marking work complete: **grep for the old symbol** and confirm remaining hits are only the deprecated alias definition or intentional external compatibility.
